AB  - Artificial intelligence is entering a new era with the advent of Large Language Models (LLMs) and Vision-Language Models (VLMs). These models facilitate efficient learning involving varied modalities and tasks such as visual question answering and image captioning. The capability of such models to carry out self-supervised, semi-supervised, and weakly supervised learning methods to integrate computer vision with natural language processing makes them competent to deal with complex tasks such as disease classification and segmentation tasks in images, cross-modal information extraction, and automatic report writing. By means of pretraining on large volumes of data without annotations, such models can work with less labeled data and improve their capacity to generalize to downstream tasks. In the context of the healthcare industry, such models address critical challenges through the integration of data obtained from various medical imaging modalities with external text-based data such as radiology reports. These have led to novel tools that have been optimized to improve detection accuracy and diagnostic processes. This review paper aims to explore the recent advances in the field of medical VLMs spanning from 2021 to 2026. The primary roles include the identification of a taxonomy to classify such models, the analysis of critical data sets essential for the assessment of such models, and the current real-world implementation. This paper also aims to identify challenges and limitations in existing tools to increase their applicability. In addition, this also outlines future pathways to expand the reach and effectiveness of VLMs in medical applications.
ER  - 

